# Public site URL used for SEO canonicals / sitemap (no trailing slash)
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://playlistbid.vercel.app

# Optional: Google Search Console HTML tag content value
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dodo Payments — the only payment rail. Copy this file to .env.local and fill
# it in. Dashboard: https://app.dodopayments.com
#
# Every one of these is server-side only. None is NEXT_PUBLIC_, and none is
# read from a client component: the browser never learns the API key, the
# webhook secret, or the product id. Placeholders only in this file — real
# credentials belong in .env.local (git-ignored) and in the Vercel project.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Dashboard → Developer → API Keys.
# Without this, /api/payments/create-checkout answers 503 and the paddle says
# card payments are not switched on rather than pretending to take money.
DODO_PAYMENTS_API_KEY=

# Dashboard → Developer → Webhooks → the endpoint's signing secret.
# Starts with whsec_. This is the single thing that lets a payment move the
# tape; without it /api/webhooks/dodo answers 503 and refuses every delivery.
DODO_PAYMENTS_WEBHOOK_KEY=

# The product a slot is billed against. Live mode has its own product list —
# a test-mode pdt_ id is not valid against live.dodopayments.com.
DODO_PAYMENTS_PRODUCT_ID=

# test_mode | live_mode  → picks test.dodopayments.com or live.dodopayments.com
DODO_PAYMENTS_ENVIRONMENT=test_mode

# How the slot price maps onto the product:
#   quantity = product priced at exactly $1.00; the server sends quantity =
#              dollars. The payer cannot edit the total, so the price the tape
#              set is the price that settles. Ceiling: whatever quantity Dodo
#              caps a cart line at, which becomes a cap on a slot's price.
#   pwyw     = product has Pay What You Want on; the server sends the amount in
#              minor units. No ceiling, but the amount box on the hosted
#              checkout is the payer's to change — an underpayment settles, the
#              guard in payment-service marks it FAILED, the tape does not move,
#              and the money has to be refunded from the dashboard by hand.
DODO_PAYMENTS_PRICING_MODE=quantity

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Going live
#
# Four variables change together, in one redeploy, or the site talks to the
# wrong host with the wrong credentials:
#   DODO_PAYMENTS_ENVIRONMENT  → live_mode
#   DODO_PAYMENTS_API_KEY      → the live-mode key
#   DODO_PAYMENTS_PRODUCT_ID   → the live-mode product
#   DODO_PAYMENTS_WEBHOOK_KEY  → the live endpoint's own signing secret
#
# Set them in the Vercel project, not here and not in .env.local: local dev
# should stay on test_mode so a stray click cannot charge a real card.
#
# Subscribe the live webhook to all eleven events the route handles. The three
# that move money back — refund.succeeded, dispute.lost, dispute.accepted —
# are the ones that lift a song off the tape; without them a refund takes the
# money back and leaves the song sitting there.
#   payment.succeeded  payment.failed  payment.cancelled  payment.processing
#   refund.succeeded   refund.failed
#   dispute.opened     dispute.expired  dispute.won  dispute.lost
#   dispute.accepted
#
# Anything that changes what settles must be off on the live product, because
# the server records its own figure and compares: a product discount, Charm
# Pricing ($10 shown as $9.99) and PPP all settle short, and a short payment is
# refused rather than treated as a cheap slot. Adaptive Currency is worse than
# that — a non-USD settlement is not comparable to a dollar figure, so the
# underpayment guard steps aside and a slot could be taken for less than it
# costs. Leave it off until the guard understands currency conversion.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Redis — required in production.
#
# The tape is one shared server-side document, because a payment webhook cannot
# write to a visitor's browser. Without Redis the tape falls back to process
# memory: fine for `npm run dev`, useless on serverless, where each instance
# gets its own copy and a redeploy wipes it. So when these are unset the site
# reports itself as not durable and the paddle refuses to take money — nobody
# is charged for a slot on a tape that is about to evaporate.
#
# Any Redis speaking the Upstash REST protocol works. Either naming is read:
#   Upstash Redis → https://console.upstash.com  (database → REST API)
#   Vercel KV     → Vercel dashboard → Storage → KV (injects KV_REST_API_*)
#
# One difference between the two pairs, on Vercel preview builds only: the
# marketplace integration attaches its KV_REST_API_* pair to Preview as well as
# Production and will not let that be narrowed, so previews would otherwise
# write to the live tape. Preview builds therefore ignore the KV_* pair and run
# on memory — not durable, so the paddle takes no money there. To give previews
# a durable tape of their own, set UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL/_TOKEN on the Preview
# environment pointing at a second database; that pair is always honoured.
#
# The same store also holds visitor stats.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# KV_REST_API_URL=
# KV_REST_API_TOKEN=
